NAME
       roffsub - a roff subset for manuals read in a
       terminal

DESCRIPTION
       A roffsub document is a man page. Every roff program
       displays it: man, nroff, groff, mandoc. It is also
       simple enough for small awk and C routines to render
       from inside a program's own binary or bash function,
       at the width of the terminal in use, with bold and
       italic where the terminal can show them. One source
       then serves as the installed page and as the
       program's built-in help.

       This page defines the source form, how it is
       rendered, and how a program presents it. The form is
       checked; content is not.

ELEMENTS
        ELEMENT         WRITTEN AS         SHOWN AS
       ---------------+------------------+------------------
        title           .TH                header and
                                           footer, optional
                                           in help
        section         .SH                bold heading at
                                           the margin
        subsection      .SS                bold heading at
                                           half an indent
        paragraph       .PP .P .LP         filled text
        definition      .TP, tag on the    tag, body
                        next line          hanging at the
                                           indent
        list item       .IP                as a definition,
                                           tag inline
        nested block    .RS .RE            margin moved in
                                           and back
        literal block   .nf .fi            lines as written
        emphasis        font escapes and   bold literals,
                        requests           italic arguments
        spacing         .sp .br            blank lines, a
                                           line break
        table           .TS .TE            open columns,
                                           horizontal rules
        comment         .\"                nothing

SOURCE FORM
       Each rule states its purpose, so it can be judged by
       whether it achieves it.

   Lexical rules
       o  Bytes 0x20 to 0x7E and newline only. A byte is
          then a column, so awk and C measure width with a
          plain length.

       o  No tab characters. Layout is stated by requests,
          so every viewer shows the same thing, and tab is
          free to separate fields in the compiled form.

       o  Line 1 is '\" t when, and only when, the page has
          a table, so that man runs tbl first. It is the
          only line that begins with an apostrophe.

       o  A control line is a period followed directly by a
          request listed below. A comment is a whole line
          beginning with a period, a backslash, and a double
          quote.

       o  In fill mode a text line begins with a word: never
          a space, never empty, and never a period or
          apostrophe unless written after \&. roff treats a
          leading space or a blank line as a layout
          instruction; stating breaks with requests gives
          them one meaning everywhere.

       o  One sentence per line is recommended. Diffs stay
          small and sentence spacing stays predictable.

   Requests
       Widths are a bare number or a number followed by n,
       counting character cells.

        REQUEST           ARGUMENTS         MEANING
       -----------------+-----------------+-----------------
        .TH               NAME SEC DATE     page title;
                          SOURCE MANUAL     first request;
                                            @DATE@ and
                                            @VERSION@ are
                                            filled in at
                                            install and
                                            compile
        .SH .SS           heading           section,
                                            subsection
        .PP .P .LP        none              new paragraph;
                                            resets the
                                            prevailing
                                            indent
        .TP               [width]           tagged
                                            paragraph; the
                                            next line, text
                                            or a font
                                            request, is the
                                            tag
        .IP               [tag [width]]     indented
                                            paragraph,
                                            optionally
                                            tagged
        .RS               [width]           move the margin
                                            in by width or
                                            by the
                                            prevailing
                                            indent
        .RE               none              restore the
                                            margin
        .nf .fi           none              literal and
                                            fill mode;
                                            literal blocks
                                            hold text only
        .B .I             words             bold or italic
                                            words
        .BR .RB .IR .RI   words             two fonts
        .BI .IB                             alternating
                                            word by word
        .sp               [N]               N blank lines,
                                            default 1
        .br               none              line break
        .TS .TE           none              table

       Double quotes group words into one argument; a quote
       inside is written \(dq. A request takes at most 9
       arguments.

   Escapes
        ESCAPE        MEANING               TERMINAL OUTPUT
       -------------+---------------------+-----------------
        \fB \fI \fR   bold, italic, roman   font change
        \fP           previous font         font change
        \-            minus, copies as a    -
                      real hyphen
        \e            backslash             \
        \&            zero-width guard      nothing
        \(space)      space that never      space
                      breaks
        \(em          em dash               --
        \(en          en dash               -
        \(bu          bullet                o
        \(aq          apostrophe            '
        \(dq          double quote          "

       The font returns to roman by the end of every request
       line and table cell, so emphasis never runs past
       where it was meant to stop.

   Tables
       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       .TE

       The options line is required and ends with a
       semicolon. It names the cell separator with tab(c), a
       printable character that is not a letter or digit and
       appears in no cell, and may add box, allbox, and
       center. Format lines follow, the last ending with a
       period; keys are l, r, and c, with modifiers b, i,
       and w(N), a minimum width. Format line i governs data
       row i and the last repeats. Each data row has one
       cell per column; a row of a single underscore is a
       rule. A cell opened with T{ continues on the
       following lines until a line beginning T}, and is
       filled within its column. roff wraps only these text
       blocks, so any cell that may need wrapping is written
       as one; the roffsub renderers wrap every cell. The
       page installed for man is written by roffsub -n,
       which rewrites each table so that roff draws it the
       same way: an empty separator column between the
       columns, rules joined by a + in those columns, no
       vertical lines, and columns holding text blocks
       widened to fill the line.

   Content
       A conventional order is NAME as name \- summary, then
       SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES,
       ENVIRONMENT, FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE ALSO. The order
       is guidance; content belongs to the author.

RENDERING

   Width and margins
       Text fills to the width with a right margin of 0, or
       to the measure when one is set. Words are packed
       greedily with a ragged right edge; a word too long
       for its line runs past the margin whole. Literal
       lines are never rewrapped. The indent unit is 7
       cells, or 4 when the width is under 60.

   Indentation and spacing
       o  A section heading sits at the margin and its body
          one unit in; a subsection heading sits half a unit
          in.

       o  Each paragraph, tagged paragraph, and indented
          paragraph is preceded by one blank line, except
          directly after a heading.

       o  A tag shorter than the prevailing indent shares
          its line with the body; a tag as long or longer
          puts the body on the next line.

       o  A width given to .TP or .IP becomes the prevailing
          indent for later items until the next .SH, .SS, or
          .PP.

       o  .RS saves the margin and prevailing indent, moves
          the margin in, and resets the prevailing indent;
          .RE restores both.

   Tables
       Tables draw no vertical lines. Each cell has one
       space of padding on each side and columns meet at one
       separator position, a space in data rows and a plus
       sign in rules, so column text sits three cells apart.
       Rules appear where the source has an underscore row;
       box adds one above and below, allbox one after every
       row.

       When a table is wider than the space available, the
       widest columns give up width first, down to a common
       cap, never below their longest word, and short
       columns keep their natural width. Cells wrap within
       their columns. When even the longest words do not
       fit, each row is shown as lines of the form header:
       value.

   Emphasis
        STYLE        USED FOR         BOLD     ITALIC
       ------------+----------------+--------+--------------
        sgr          a terminal       ESC[1m   ESC[4m,
                                               underline
        overstrike   a pager          c BS c   _ BS c
        plain        a file, a        none     none
                     pipe,
                     TERM=dumb
        pre          pre-formatting   \fB      \fI

       Control bytes are added only as a line is written, so
       they never count toward its width. Emphasis is closed
       before every newline and reopened on the next line.
       The SGR parameters may be replaced, so that bold and
       italic show as colors.

PROGRAM INTERFACE

   Help
       --help presents the full manual. What it writes is
       fixed by four inputs: the embedded manual, the width,
       the style, and the destination. The C and bash forms
       of a program write the same bytes for the same
       inputs.

        OUTPUT         GOES TO        STYLE        WIDTH
       --------------+--------------+------------+----------
        terminal       the pager      overstrike   terminal
        terminal,      the terminal   sgr          terminal
        PAGER is cat
        file or pipe   the file or    plain        80
                       pipe

       The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less,
       otherwise more. The width is MANWIDTH when set,
       otherwise COLUMNS, otherwise the terminal's own.
       ROFFSUB_STYLE, ROFFSUB_SGR, and ROFFSUB_MEASURE
       override the style, the emphasis parameters, and the
       measure; TERM=dumb selects plain. ROFFSUB_HEADING
       moves section headings that many columns in from the
       margin, leaving everything else in place.

   Usage
       -h serves a different purpose from the manual: a
       quick reference, a refresher and confirmation when a
       full review is not needed. It is composed for that
       purpose, not extracted from the manual.

   Profiles
       A man page is built from the source and installed in
       every case. Inside the program the manual is carried
       in one of three profiles.

        PROFILE        THE PROGRAM         HELP GIVES
                       CARRIES
       --------------+-------------------+------------------
        full           records and the     any width,
                       renderer            emphasis by
                                           terminal
        preformatted   text set at 80      fixed width,
                       columns, fonts      emphasis by
                       kept, and the       terminal
                       emitter
        plain          text set at 80      fixed width
                       columns

       The full profile is used when the renderer is no
       larger than the program it would live in, not
       counting the manual, and an exported bash function
       stays within its size budget; otherwise preformatted.
       Plain is used only when a program asks for it.

COMPILED FORM
       One record per line: an operation, then fields
       separated by tabs. The operations are th, sh, ss, pp,
       tp, ip, rs, re, nf, fi, tx, sp, br, and for tables
       ts, tf, tr, t_, and te; the first record is v 1. Text
       fields carry only \fB, \fI, \fR, \\, and \~, and end
       in roman. Records are passed to awk on its standard
       input, never with -v, which would rewrite their
       backslashes.

CONFORMANCE
        ID    RULE
       -----+-----------------------------------------------
        E01   byte outside 0x20 to 0x7E
        E02   request not in roffsub, or an apostrophe
              control line
        E03   escape not in roffsub
        E04   unbalanced .nf/.fi, .RS/.RE, .TS/.TE, or
              T{/T}
        E05   fill-mode text line empty or beginning with a
              space
        E06   font not roman at a request line or cell end
        E07   the table line and the tables disagree
        E08   table form
        E09   .TH missing or not the first request
        E10   request arguments
        E11   .TP without a tag line
        W01   option hyphen written without \-
        W02   abbreviation ending a line without \&
        W03   table wider than 71 columns with no text
              blocks to wrap

EXAMPLES
       Each example shows its source, then the same source
       rendered in place.

   Open table with a header rule
       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       \fB\-k\fR \fIkey\fR@sort on \fIkey\fR
       .TE

        OPTION   MEANING
       --------+-------------------
        -r       reverse the order
        -k key   sort on key

   Boxed table, aligned columns
       The box option adds rules above and below; keys r and
       c align right and center.

       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb cb rb
       l  c  r.
       NAME@KIND@SIZE
       _
       alpha@file@12
       beta@directory@3456
       .TE

       -------+-----------+------
        NAME      KIND      SIZE
       -------+-----------+------
        alpha     file        12
        beta    directory   3456
       -------+-----------+------

   Ruled table, centered
       The allbox option puts a rule after every row; center
       places the table in the middle of the line.

       .TS
       allbox center tab(@);
       cb cb
       r  l.
       CODE@MEANING
       0@success
       1@a check found errors
       2@a usage or input error
       .TE

                  ------+------------------------
                   CODE          MEANING
                  ------+------------------------
                      0   success
                  ------+------------------------
                      1   a check found errors
                  ------+------------------------
                      2   a usage or input error
                  ------+------------------------

   Text blocks that wrap
       A cell written between T{ and T} wraps within its
       column, in roff and in the roffsub renderers alike.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       TERM@DEFINITION
       _
       measure@T{
       the longest line that filled text may reach,
       whatever the width of the terminal
       T}
       prevailing indent@T{
       the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use
       when they give none of their own
       T}
       .TE

        TERM                DEFINITION
       -------------------+---------------------------------
        measure             the longest line that filled
                            text may reach, whatever the
                            width of the terminal
        prevailing indent   the indent that tagged and
                            indented paragraphs use when
                            they give none of their own

   Minimum column width
       A key with w(N) keeps its column at least N cells
       wide.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lw(12) l.
       key@value
       .TE

        key            value

   Definitions with a narrow indent
       A width on .TP sets the prevailing indent for the
       items that follow.

       .TP 4
       .B a
       first
       .TP
       .B b
       second

       a   first

       b   second

   Numbered list and nested block
       .IP 1. 4
       Write the page.
       .IP 2.
       Check it:
       .RS
       .nf
       roffsub \-c page.1
       .fi
       .RE

       1.  Write the page.

       2.  Check it:
           roffsub -c page.1

   Emphasis
       .BR roffsub (1)
       reads \fIfile\fR with \fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR.

       roffsub(1) reads file with -w width.

SEE ALSO
       roffsub(1), man(7), tbl(1)
